data.content:
# EF Core 10 Breaking Changes for Your Code

## 1. `SetProperty` — value overload removed

EF Core 10 removed the `SetProperty(Expression<Func<T,P>>, P value)` overload. The value must now be an `Expression<Func<TSource, TProperty>>` lambda. Your `Expression.Constant("foo")` no longer resolves.

**Fix:** Wrap the constant in a lambda expression:

```csharp
var sParameter = Expression.Parameter(typeof(SetPropertyCalls<Blog>), "s");
var blogParameter = Expression.Parameter(typeof(Blog), "b");

// Wrap constant in a lambda: _ => "foo"
var dummyParam = Expression.Parameter(typeof(Blog), "_");
var valueLambda = Expression.Lambda<Func<Blog, string>>(
    Expression.Constant("foo"), dummyParam);

setters = Expression.Lambda<Func<SetPropertyCalls<Blog>, SetPropertyCalls<Blog>>>(
    Expression.Call(
        instance: setters.Body,
        methodName: nameof(SetPropertyCalls<Blog>.SetProperty),
        typeArguments: new[] { typeof(string) },
        arguments: new Expression[]
        {
            Expression.Lambda<Func<Blog, string>>(
                Expression.Property(blogParameter, nameof(Blog.Name)), blogParameter),
            valueLambda  // was: Expression.Constant("foo")
        }),
    setters.Parameters);
```

## 2. Complex types — ambiguous property access now throws

EF Core 10 no longer silently picks one complex-type path when the same property name exists on multiple complex properties. Accessing `c.ShippingAddress.Street` vs `c.BillingAddress.Street` is fine, but if you ever used a shared projection or anonymous mapping that was ambiguous, it now throws `InvalidOperationException`.

**Fix:** Always access leaf properties through the full complex-property path explicitly:

```csharp
// ✅ Unambiguous — qualify through the complex property
context.Customers
    .Select(c => new {
        Ship = c.ShippingAddress.Street,   // explicit path
        Bill = c.BillingAddress.Street     // explicit path
    });

// For nested: use the full chain
context.Orders
    .Select(o => o.Payment.BillingAddress.City);
```

If you have `HasColumnName` annotations that collide, give each complex property's leaf a distinct column name:

```csharp
b.ComplexProperty(c => c.ShippingAddress, a => {
    a.Property(x => x.Street).HasColumnName("ShippingStreet");
});
b.ComplexProperty(c => c.BillingAddress, a => {
    a.Property(x => x.Street).HasColumnName("BillingStreet");
});
```

## 3. `IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger` — new `CommandSource` parameter

EF Core 10 added `CommandSource commandSource` to the interception methods. Your implementation no longer satisfies the interface.

**Fix:**

```csharp
public class MyLogger : IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger
{
    public InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> CommandReaderExecuting(
        IRelationalConnection connection,
        DbCommand command,
        DbContext context,
        Guid commandId,
        Guid connectionId,
        DateTimeOffset startTime,
        CommandSource commandSource)   // ← new parameter
    { /* logging */ }
}
```

---

**Summary of actions:**
| Piece | What broke | Fix |
|-------|-----------|-----|
| `ExecuteUpdateAsync` expression tree | Constant-value `SetProperty` overload removed | Wrap value in `Expression.Lambda<Func<T,P>>` |
| Complex types | Ambiguous property access throws at runtime | Fully qualify through complex-property navigation |
| Diagnostics logger | Interface added `CommandSource` param | Add the parameter to your implementation |
